arti: no `AtomicU64` on targets without 64-bit atomics (32-bit PowerPC)

mrustc on powerpc-apple-darwin, building arti 2.7.0 (`--package arti`):

    crates/tor-async-utils/src/bw_pool/bucket.rs:16:5-46 error:0: Unable to resolve `use` target ::"std-0_0_0"::sync::atomic::AtomicU64

32-bit PowerPC, MIPS and older ARM have no 64-bit atomics, so `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64`
does not exist there (`cfg(target_has_atomic = "64")` is unset). Five workspace crates use it
outside tests:

- tor-async-utils bw_pool: the token bucket's `available` count and a waiter's `granted` grant
  (fetch_update, swap, load, store);
- tor-proto util/ts.rs `AtomicOptTimestamp` (coarsetime ticks: compare_exchange, fetch_max,
  load, store; also a `static` via its `const fn new`, and `Default`/`Debug` derives), conflux
  `last_seq_delivered` (load, fetch_add), `TunnelId` counter;
- tor-chanmgr `UniqPendingChanId`, tor-guardmgr `RequestId`, tor-circmgr `IsolationToken`:
  `static` u64 ID counters (fetch_add).

All of them need the full 64 bits (tick counts, u64 IDs whose exhaustion is asserted), so a new
`tor_async_utils::atomic64::AtomicU64` (the other four crates depend on tor-async-utils; it lives
there rather than in tor-basic-utils so that no crate built before this stop changes)
re-exports `std`'s type where the target has 64-bit atomics, and is otherwise a `Mutex<u64>`
with the same `const fn new`, `Default`, `Debug` and the eight methods above. The orderings are
accepted and ignored: each operation holds the lock. Other targets compile exactly the code
they did. tor-proto's xon_xoff reader.rs uses `AtomicU64` in a `#[cfg(test)]` module only and is
left alone; tests/benches/examples are not built by the port.

Vendored crates in the default `arti` build were scanned too: they either gate their 64-bit
atomics (tokio, crossbeam, bitvec/radium, figment via the `atomic` crate, serde, bytemuck, ...),
use portable-atomic (moka, metrics), or are optional and off by default
(metrics-exporter-prometheus = `metrics`, opentelemetry_sdk). coarsetime has its own
crate_patches (coarsetime@0.1.32+.patch).

Applies to the GitLab arti-v2.7.0 tree (arti-arti-v2.7.0/) with -p1 (patch.pre_args-replace -p0 -p1).

diff -Nru a/crates/tor-async-utils/src/atomic64.rs b/crates/tor-async-utils/src/atomic64.rs
--- a/crates/tor-async-utils/src/atomic64.rs	1970-01-01 00:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ b/crates/tor-async-utils/src/atomic64.rs	2026-11-06 03:52:14.309762292 +0000
@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+//! `AtomicU64` for targets without 64-bit atomics.
+//!
+//! 32-bit PowerPC, MIPS and older ARM have no `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64`.
+//! There, [`AtomicU64`] is a `u64` behind a mutex with the subset of the
+//! `AtomicU64` API that Arti uses; everywhere else it is `std`'s type.
+
+#[cfg(target_has_atomic = "64")]
+pub use std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64;
+
+#[cfg(not(target_has_atomic = "64"))]
+pub use fallback::AtomicU64;
+
+/// The mutex-based `AtomicU64`.
+#[cfg(not(target_has_atomic = "64"))]
+mod fallback {
+    use std::fmt;
+    use std::sync::atomic::Ordering;
+    use std::sync::{Mutex, MutexGuard};
+
+    /// A `u64` behind a mutex, standing in for `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64`.
+    ///
+    /// Every operation holds the lock, so it is at least as strong as any
+    /// [`Ordering`] Arti asks for; the orderings are accepted and ignored.
+    #[derive(Default)]
+    pub struct AtomicU64(Mutex<u64>);
+
+    impl AtomicU64 {
+        /// See `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::new`.
+        pub const fn new(value: u64) -> Self {
+            Self(Mutex::new(value))
+        }
+
+        /// Lock the value, ignoring poisoning: a `u64` has no invariant to break.
+        fn lock(&self) -> MutexGuard<'_, u64> {
+            self.0.lock().unwrap_or_else(|poisoned| poisoned.into_inner())
+        }
+
+        /// See `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::load`.
+        pub fn load(&self, _order: Ordering) -> u64 {
+            *self.lock()
+        }
+
+        /// See `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::store`.
+        pub fn store(&self, value: u64, _order: Ordering) {
+            *self.lock() = value;
+        }
+
+        /// See `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::swap`.
+        pub fn swap(&self, value: u64, _order: Ordering) -> u64 {
+            std::mem::replace(&mut *self.lock(), value)
+        }
+
+        /// See `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::fetch_add`.
+        pub fn fetch_add(&self, value: u64, _order: Ordering) -> u64 {
+            let mut cur = self.lock();
+            let prev = *cur;
+            *cur = prev.wrapping_add(value);
+            prev
+        }
+
+        /// See `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::fetch_max`.
+        pub fn fetch_max(&self, value: u64, _order: Ordering) -> u64 {
+            let mut cur = self.lock();
+            let prev = *cur;
+            *cur = prev.max(value);
+            prev
+        }
+
+        /// See `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::compare_exchange`.
+        pub fn compare_exchange(
+            &self,
+            current: u64,
+            new: u64,
+            _success: Ordering,
+            _failure: Ordering,
+        ) -> Result<u64, u64> {
+            let mut cur = self.lock();
+            let prev = *cur;
+            if prev == current {
+                *cur = new;
+                Ok(prev)
+            } else {
+                Err(prev)
+            }
+        }
+
+        /// See `std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64::fetch_update`.
+        pub fn fetch_update<F>(
+            &self,
+            _set_order: Ordering,
+            _fetch_order: Ordering,
+            mut f: F,
+        ) -> Result<u64, u64>
+        where
+            F: FnMut(u64) -> Option<u64>,
+        {
+            let mut cur = self.lock();
+            let prev = *cur;
+            match f(prev) {
+                Some(next) => {
+                    *cur = next;
+                    Ok(prev)
+                }
+                None => Err(prev),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    impl fmt::Debug for AtomicU64 {
+        fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+            fmt::Debug::fmt(&self.load(Ordering::Relaxed), f)
+        }
+    }
+}
